dialect grammar
grammar stdio.obj.snapshot
extension obj
start document
comment none

# P2-FG1: real Wavefront OBJ 3.0 keyword-statement grammar, replacing the old ABNF placeholder
# this file used to contain (unparseable by the repo's own `parse_grammar` — wrong header
# dialect, `;` comments, `/`-alternation, `%x` char-class ranges all outside this dialect's
# lexer alphabet). Mirrors this artifact's own real `decode_obj`/`parse_face_vertex`
# (⚙️engine/component.rs:81-218/57-74) exactly: statements may appear in ANY order (real OBJ
# files interleave v/vt/vn/f/o/g/usemtl/mtllib/s freely) — this is the real, commonly-implemented
# grammar the DECODER accepts, not the ENCODER's own documented normal-form emission order (see
# that module's own doc comment for the distinction; this grammar only needs to recognize real
# `print_dsl` output, which already follows the documented order, a strict subset of what this
# grammar accepts).
#
# `comment none` (P2-M1 item 4): real OBJ `#`-comment lines are NOT framework-level comment
# trivia here — `decode_obj` retains them verbatim in `unknown_statements` (line 106-109),
# exactly like any other unrecognized statement line, and `encode_obj` re-emits them in the
# trailer. Declaring `comment none` stops the shared lexer from silently stripping `#...` content
# before this grammar ever sees it, so `unknown-stmt` below can genuinely capture it — the same
# reasoning `stdio.csv`/`stdio.txt`'s own `comment none` directives document for their own
# legitimately-`#`-containing real content.
document = statement*
statement = vertex-stmt | texcoord-stmt | normal-stmt | face-stmt | object-stmt | group-stmt | usemtl-stmt | mtllib-stmt | smoothing-stmt | unknown-stmt

# `v x y z [w]` — decode_obj:112-118. Rust's own `f64` `Display` (`fmt_f64`-equivalent `{}`
# formatting `encode_obj` uses throughout) prints a whole-valued float WITHOUT a decimal point
# (`1.0` -> `"1"`), so every coordinate below accepts INT as well as FLOAT — not merely FLOAT —
# matching real emitted text, not just the RFC-shaped literal.
number = FLOAT | INT
vertex-stmt = "v" number number number number?
# `vt u [v] [w]` — decode_obj:119-124; encode_obj always emits BOTH u and v (only w is ever
# omitted — ObjTexCoord.v is a plain, always-present f64, never optional at the wire level).
texcoord-stmt = "vt" number number number?
# `vn x y z` — decode_obj:125-129; always exactly 3 components, both directions.
normal-stmt = "vn" number number number

# `f <face_vertex>+` (>= 3 per decode_obj:136-138); the dialect has no "at least N" quantifier —
# modeled as 3 required occurrences then a `*` tail, matching real >=3-vertex n-gon faces exactly.
face-stmt = "f" face-vertex face-vertex face-vertex face-vertex*
# `v`, `v/vt`, `v//vn`, `v/vt/vn` — parse_face_vertex:57-74. Longest-alternative-first ordering:
# an alternation tries alternatives in order and commits to the first that matches its own full
# symbol sequence, so the 2-slash forms must precede the 1-slash/bare forms or a bare `INT` would
# wrongly swallow just the leading index of a slash-separated triplet.
face-vertex = INT "/" INT "/" INT | INT "/" "/" INT | INT "/" INT | INT

# `o [name]` — decode_obj:164-166 (`cur_active_object = parts.next()...`, i.e. a single token, but
# may legitimately be absent). Per this ticket's own W0 census gap: `o`/`g` names are real
# "rest-of-line" content (arbitrary, possibly multi-token per `g`'s own real semantics below) —
# M1's `LINE` raw-span terminal (📖️grammar/🦀️.rs's own worked example literally names
# "obj's o/g names") captures this verbatim, past whatever the shared lexer would otherwise
# fragment an unusual name into. A bare `LINE` may legitimately be empty (no name).
object-stmt = "o" LINE
# `g [name]*` — decode_obj:167-169 (`cur_active_groups = parts.map(...).collect()`, i.e. ZERO OR
# MORE space-separated names active simultaneously) — same `LINE` rest-of-line capture.
group-stmt = "g" LINE
# `usemtl [material]` — decode_obj:170-178 (a single optional token per real parsing, but a
# hand-crafted `SetUsemtl`/mutation-driven material name could contain arbitrary text never
# produced by the real text decoder — `LINE` is the honest superset that always structurally
# recognizes real `usemtl <name>\n` output either way).
usemtl-stmt = "usemtl" LINE
# `mtllib <name> [name]*` — decode_obj:179-184 (ALL remaining tokens rejoined with a single space
# — `rest.join(" ")` — genuinely multi-word library-name lists are real content here).
mtllib-stmt = "mtllib" LINE
# `s off|<N>` — decode_obj:185-195; encode_obj:296-305 always emits exactly one of the two forms.
smoothing-stmt = "s" {"off" | INT}

# Catch-all: any physical line not matched by an earlier alternative — `#`-comment lines AND
# genuinely-unrecognized keyword lines alike, decode_obj:106-109/196-198 both fold into the SAME
# `unknown_statements` sink with no distinction beyond the raw text, so one shared fallback
# production models both honestly (the "nothing silently dropped" rule this ticket's own brief
# names) — must stay the LAST alternative in `statement` so the specific keyword productions above
# get first refusal.
unknown-stmt = LINE
